---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../components/CaseStudyLayout.astro";

const title = "Modernizing Tomahawk’s Android App — Derek DiLeo";
const description =
  "How I evolved a real-time Android vehicle status feature from Java and XML into testable, reusable Compose architecture.";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  backHref="/#approach"
  eyebrow="Android · Kotlin · Jetpack Compose"
  heading="Modernizing Tomahawk’s Android app, one feature at a time."
  intro="I evolved a real-time vehicle status card from imperative Java and XML into a reusable, testable feature built around ViewModels, Kotlin Flows, domain use cases, and Jetpack Compose."
>
  <div slot="meta" class="case-study__meta">
        <span>Role</span>
        <strong>Android engineer · UI architecture owner</strong>
        <span>Scope</span>
        <strong>Incremental modernization</strong>
  </div>

  <div slot="aside">
    <p class="eyebrow">At a glance</p>
    <dl>
      <div><dt>Feature passes</dt><dd>2</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Coordinate formats</dt><dd>5</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tests added by the team</dt><dd>3,000+</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Shared location use case</dt><dd>2 cards</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>

        <p class="eyebrow">The starting point</p>
        <h2>A real-time feature in a legacy UI.</h2>
        <p>
          When I joined the team, the application was exclusively imperative
          Java. Observer patterns were common, with
          LiveData and Android Observable fields carrying state through the UI.
          Data access often
          went through static singleton calls such as{" "}
          <code>EntityDatabase.instance.getFoo()</code>. The AgentStatusCard
          used an XML layout with view binding and a Java state holder.
        </p>
        <p>
          My first production use of Jetpack Compose came through automated
          vehicle-pairing wizards. These focused workflows were separate
          Activities launched from the main application, replacing a
          CLI-driven pairing process with guided automation. They gave me a
          contained place to establish Compose and ViewModel patterns. Once the
          approach worked, I used that foundation to convince the team to move
          the main application toward Jetpack and modern Android development.
        </p>
        <p>
          The card is the operator’s view of the vehicle currently being
          controlled. It combines
          live position, ground speed, throttle position when available, energy
          state, signal ratio, vehicle identity, and control mode in one compact
          surface. Small changes in the underlying node data can affect several
          pieces of UI at once, so the feature was a useful place to establish
          a better pattern.
        </p>

        <p class="eyebrow">Pass one</p>
        <h2>Modernize the surface without stopping delivery.</h2>
        <p>
          The first pass introduced a ViewModel and Jetpack Compose while
          preserving the feature’s existing behavior. The ViewModel became the
          boundary between the data being observed and the UI state rendered by
          Compose, replacing the direct coupling of the old XML implementation.
          The Composable obtains that ViewModel through Hilt, keeping
          construction and dependencies outside the UI itself.
        </p>
        <p>
          This was an incremental change rather than a rewrite. The application
          is large and still uses a hybrid navigation and UI stack, so features
          are being migrated one piece at a time. New development is Compose
          and ViewModel-first, while existing screens move forward through
          carefully bounded interop.
        </p>

        <p class="eyebrow">Pass two</p>
        <h2>Turn the feature into a pattern.</h2>
        <p>
          About a year later, after deepening my experience with Kotlin,
          architecture, and modern Android practices, I revisited the card. I
          extracted business logic into domain-layer use cases and added Kotlin
          unit tests alongside Jetpack Compose UI tests.
        </p>
        <p>
          Node data is exposed as a Flow. When the selected vehicle changes,
          the Flow emits and the ViewModel updates only the UI state affected by
          that change. Position formatting is handled by{" "}
          <code>ObserveLocationUseCase(locationId)</code>, which returns the
          current location as a string in the user’s preferred format: DD, DMS,
          MGRS, DMDM, or UTM.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same use case was later reused by UserStatusCard for the
          operator’s own position, ground speed, and altitude. Both cards now
          share the same coordinate-formatting behavior; the call site simply
          supplies a different location ID.
        </p>

        <p class="eyebrow">The result</p>
        <h2>Safer changes, faster feedback.</h2>
        <p>
          Future changes are easier because new work follows a standard pattern:
          observe the relevant data, map it into explicit, immutable UI state,
          and render that state with Compose. Shared behavior lives in reusable
          domain use cases instead of being duplicated across screens.
        </p>
        <p>
          Regression risk is lower because the feature is covered at both the
          unit and UI levels. In under a year, my team and I grew the
          Java/Kotlin unit-test suite from zero tests to more than 3,000, and
          that coverage continues to grow. It makes it possible to change
          architecture and behavior with confidence while continuing to deliver
          product work.
        </p>

        <p class="eyebrow">What I would do next</p>
        <h2>Make the migration path intentional from day one.</h2>
        <p>
          If I were starting this work today, I would establish the domain and
          UI-state contracts before migrating the first screen, then use those
          contracts to guide each incremental feature pass. I would also define
          test seams around platform data earlier so new screens could begin
          with fakes and deterministic tests instead of adding them later.
        </p>
  
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .case-study {
    padding-top: 10rem;
    padding-bottom: 7rem;
  }
  .back-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  .case-study__hero {
    max-width: 60rem;
    margin: 5rem 0 4rem;
  }
  h1 {
    max-width: 11ch;
    margin: 1rem 0 1.75rem;
    font-size: clamp(3.5rem, 9vw, 8rem);
    line-height: 0.92;
    letter-spacing: -0.07em;
  }
  .case-study__intro {
    max-width: 40rem;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .case-study__meta {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, max-content);
    gap: 0.5rem 1.2rem;
    margin-top: 3rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font: 0.68rem/1.3 var(--font-mono);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .case-study__meta strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .case-study__body {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(12rem, 0.6fr) minmax(0, 1.4fr);
    gap: clamp(3rem, 10vw, 9rem);
    margin-top: 7rem;
  }
  .case-study__aside {
    position: sticky;
    top: 8rem;
    align-self: start;
  }
  dl {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
  }
  dl div {
    padding: 1rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  }
  dt {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font: 0.62rem/1.3 var(--font-mono);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  dd {
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }
  .case-study__prose {
    max-width: 42rem;
  }
  .case-study__prose .eyebrow:not(:first-child) {
    margin-top: 5rem;
  }
  .case-study__prose h2 {
    max-width: 15ch;
    margin: 0.8rem 0 1.5rem;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.055em;
  }
  .case-study__prose p:not(.eyebrow) {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.85;
  }
  code {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font: 0.9em var(--font-mono);
  }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .case-study {
      padding-top: 8rem;
    }
    .case-study__hero {
      margin-top: 4rem;
    }
    .case-study__meta {
      grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr;
    }
    .case-study__body {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      margin-top: 5rem;
    }
    .case-study__aside {
      position: static;
    }
  }
</style>
